    "content": "Green card holders are generally secure but face increased risks of detention or deportation if they have criminal convictions (including minor drug offenses), have spent over six months outside the U.S., or engaged in activities deemed a security risk. Enhanced vetting, particularly concerning social media and international travel, has increased vulnerability. Key Risks for Green Card Holders (2025-2026) Criminal Convictions: Even minor offenses—specifically drug offenses (including marijuana), gun offenses, domestic violence, child abuse, or crimes involving \"moral turpitude\" (fraud/theft)—can trigger deportation. Extended Travel: Being outside the U.S. for more than 180 days can make you subject to intense scrutiny or deemed to have abandoned residency. \"Extreme Vetting\": Increased scrutiny at airports and public safety reviews by USCIS have led to higher detention rates, particularly for those with questionable records. Social Media/Political Activity: Federal agencies are actively monitoring online activity for national security concerns. Fraud: If fraud is discovered in the original application, USCIS may revoke status. Protective Measures Carry Documents: Always carry your physical Green Card. Travel Precautions: Avoid trips longer than six months. If planning long travel, obtain a re-entry permit (Form I-131). Legal Counsel: Consult an immigration attorney before traveling if you have any criminal record. Disclaimer: Immigration laws are complex and subject to change. For personalized advice, consult an qualified attorney.",
